# Where your MSYS2 lives.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (beside this one) and set the path. `.env` is
# git-ignored on purpose: which MSYS2 tree you use is a property of your
# machine, not of the repository, so it must never be committed.
#
#   copy .env.example .env
#
# You only need this when discovery guesses wrong. The installer, the test
# suite and the shim all find MSYS2 in this order:
#
#   1. the DSH_MSYS2_ROOT environment variable
#   2. this file
#   3. $GITHUB_WORKSPACE\msys\msys64, the layout CI installs into
#   4. msys2_shell.cmd anywhere on PATH
#   5. the usual places: %USERPROFILE%\Downloads\msys64, %USERPROFILE%\msys64,
#      C:\msys64, C:\tools\msys64
#
# So a machine with MSYS2 on PATH, or at %USERPROFILE%\Downloads\msys64, needs
# no .env at all. Write one when you keep MSYS2 somewhere else, or keep more
# than one and want to pin which.
#
# Setting DSH_MSYS2_ROOT in the environment overrides this file for one shell
# or one command. The installer also writes this file itself when you pick a
# path with its Browse button, so a manual pick is remembered.

# The directory containing msys2_shell.cmd. Point at the install root.
#
# The value may also be the msys2_shell.cmd file itself, may carry a trailing
# separator, and may be quoted. Backslashes need no escaping, and are safest
# unquoted:
#
#   DSH_MSYS2_ROOT=C:\msys64
#
# A path with spaces is best written quoted, with single quotes to keep
# backslashes literal:
#
#   DSH_MSYS2_ROOT='C:\Program Files\msys64'
DSH_MSYS2_ROOT=C:\msys64

# Accepted aliases, checked only if DSH_MSYS2_ROOT is unset or empty:
#
#   MSYS2_ROOT=C:\msys64
#   DSH_MSYS2_HOME=C:\msys64
#
# Values are NOT interpolated: a $ or % in a path is taken literally, as is a
# Windows environment variable reference. Write the path out in full.
